    {
      "@id": "x:n1",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Study",
        "Study"
      ],
      "title": "Longitudinal analysis of serum samples from 84 individuals before SLE classification and 86 matched healthy controls measuring autoantibodies and soluble immune mediators",
      "content": "Longitudinal analysis of serum samples from 84 individuals before SLE classification and 86 matched healthy controls measuring autoantibodies and soluble immune mediators\n\nSamples from the Department of Defense Serum Repository (DoDSR) collected prior to and at/after SLE classification (average 5.98 years pre-classification). Cases had average 2.96 samples, controls average 2 samples. 416 total samples analyzed. Samples divided into four time periods relative to SLE classification for longitudinal analysis.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n1"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n2",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) for BLyS (R&D Systems) and APRIL (eBioscience/Affymetrix) per manufacturer's protocol",
      "content": "Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) for BLyS (R&D Systems) and APRIL (eBioscience/Affymetrix) per manufacturer's protocol\n\nSerum levels of BLyS and APRIL assessed using ELISA kits from R&D Systems (Minneapolis, MN) and eBioscience/Affymetrix (San Diego, CA) respectively, following manufacturer protocols.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n2"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n3",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "xMAP multiplex assays (eBioscience/Affymetrix) for 30 immune mediators including cytokines, chemokines, and soluble TNFR superfamily members",
      "content": "xMAP multiplex assays (eBioscience/Affymetrix) for 30 immune mediators including cytokines, chemokines, and soluble TNFR superfamily members\n\nNormalized fluorescence intensity values for 30 immune mediators determined by xMAP multiplex assays. Quality control performed; four mediators (IFN-α, TNF-α, IL-10, IL-15) excluded due to >50% measurements below detection limit. Average inter-assay CV 10.5%, intra-assay CV <10%.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n3"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n4",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "BioPlex 2200 system (Bio-Rad Technologies) for simultaneous detection of autoantibody specificities: dsDNA, chromatin, Ro/SSA, La/SSB, Sm, SmRNP, and RNP",
      "content": "BioPlex 2200 system (Bio-Rad Technologies) for simultaneous detection of autoantibody specificities: dsDNA, chromatin, Ro/SSA, La/SSB, Sm, SmRNP, and RNP\n\nSemi-quantitative values for anti-dsDNA reported as IU/mL (positive ≥10 IU/mL). All other autoantibody specificities reported in autoantibody index (AI) units based on fluorescence intensity (range 0-8) using manufacturer-specified positive cutoff (positive ≥1 AI). Factor XIIIb levels evaluated as quality control.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n4"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n5",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Mixed linear regression models fitted on normalized FI values of each soluble mediator over time using lme4 package in R (version 2.15.0) with intercepts as random effects and disease status as fixed…",
      "content": "Mixed linear regression models fitted on normalized FI values of each soluble mediator over time using lme4 package in R (version 2.15.0) with intercepts as random effects and disease status as fixed effect\n\nUsed to assess longitudinal changes in soluble mediator levels. Non-parametric rank-based analysis with GraphPad Prism 6.0 for asymmetric distributions. P-values adjusted by false discovery rate (FDR) using fdrtools package. Kaplan-Meier survival curves for timing of dysregulation. Cox proportional hazard models for hazard ratios. Random forest classification with randomForest R package (ntree=2000, mtry=sqrt(p), importance=TRUE, proximity=TRUE). Stepwise variable selection using Genuer and Tuleau-Malot algorithm.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n5"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n6",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Question",
        "Question"
      ],
      "title": "What immune dysregulation beyond autoantibody accumulation contributes to preclinical SLE pathogenesis and transition to clinical disease?",
      "content": "What immune dysregulation beyond autoantibody accumulation contributes to preclinical SLE pathogenesis and transition to clinical disease?\n\nThe paper investigates whether soluble mediators from innate and adaptive immune pathways are dysregulated prior to SLE classification and whether they improve prognostic accuracy over autoantibodies alone.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n6"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n7",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Question",
        "Question"
      ],
      "title": "What is the temporal relationship between soluble mediator dysregulation and autoantibody accumulation during preclinical SLE development?",
      "content": "What is the temporal relationship between soluble mediator dysregulation and autoantibody accumulation during preclinical SLE development?\n\nThe study compares timing of autoantibody specificity detection and soluble mediator dysregulation as patients approach SLE classification.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n7"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n8",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Claim",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Multiple soluble mediators including IL-5, IL-6, and IFN-γ are significantly elevated in pre-SLE individuals more than 3.5 years before classification, prior to or concurrent with autoantibody positi…",
      "content": "Multiple soluble mediators including IL-5, IL-6, and IFN-γ are significantly elevated in pre-SLE individuals more than 3.5 years before classification, prior to or concurrent with autoantibody positivity.\n\nIn the earliest time period (>3.5 years pre-classification), cases showed elevated IL-6 (q=6.83×10⁻⁶), IL-12p70 (q=0.013), IFN-γ (q=0.035), IL-4 (q=0.01), IL-5 (q=3.6×10⁻⁶), IP-10 (q=0.023), and decreased TGF-β (q=0.023) compared to matched controls.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n8"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n9",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Claim",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Innate cytokines, IFN-associated chemokines, and soluble TNF superfamily mediators increase longitudinally in pre-SLE cases approaching classification but not in healthy controls.",
      "content": "Innate cytokines, IFN-associated chemokines, and soluble TNF superfamily mediators increase longitudinally in pre-SLE cases approaching classification but not in healthy controls.\n\nCases gained an average of 0.5 dysregulated mediators per year vs 0.06 in controls (p<0.001). Significant longitudinal increases in IP-10 (p<0.001), MIG (p<0.001), IL-2 (p=0.008), IL-5 (p=0.001), IL-21 (p=0.007), BLyS (p=0.008), APRIL (p=0.013), TNFRI (p<0.001), TNFRII (p<0.001).",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n9"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n10",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Claim",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "BLyS and APRIL levels are comparable in pre-SLE cases and controls until less than 10 months before classification, then increase significantly in cases.",
      "content": "BLyS and APRIL levels are comparable in pre-SLE cases and controls until less than 10 months before classification, then increase significantly in cases.\n\nBLyS and APRIL were not significantly different >3.5 years pre-classification (q=0.927 and q=0.672) but became significantly elevated in the period 0.9 years before to 0.1 years after classification (q=0.003 and q=0.019). Mixed models confirmed significant longitudinal increases in cases vs controls (BLyS p=0.008, APRIL p=0.013).",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n10"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n11",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Claim",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Dysregulation of adaptive immune mediators (IL-4, IL-5, IL-6, IFN-γ, MIG) precedes autoantibody accumulation in preclinical SLE.",
      "content": "Dysregulation of adaptive immune mediators (IL-4, IL-5, IL-6, IFN-γ, MIG) precedes autoantibody accumulation in preclinical SLE.\n\nEach of these mediators was elevated in >50% of cases by 2 years pre-classification and 85-95% by 2 years post-classification. Hazard ratios show mediator dysregulation significantly more likely than autoantibody positivity for all tested autoantibodies (anti-dsDNA, anti-chromatin, anti-Ro/SSA, anti-La/SSB, anti-RNP, anti-Sm).",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n11"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n12",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Claim",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Random forest models incorporating ANA and anti-Ro/SSA positivity with IL-5, IL-6, and MIG levels distinguish future SLE patients with 92% accuracy across the entire pre-classification period, compar…",
      "content": "Random forest models incorporating ANA and anti-Ro/SSA positivity with IL-5, IL-6, and MIG levels distinguish future SLE patients with 92% accuracy across the entire pre-classification period, compared to 78% accuracy using ANA positivity alone.\n\nIn the combined model spanning all pre-classification time: sensitivity 0.93 (0.87-0.97), specificity 0.89 (0.77-0.96), PPV 0.96 (0.90-0.99), NPV 0.84 (0.71-0.93), accuracy 92% (±1.78%). ANA-only model: accuracy 78% (±2.42%). Soluble mediators alone (IP-10, IL-5, IL-6): accuracy 84% (±2.95%).",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n12"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n13",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Claim",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "A distinct ANA-negative but IL-5/IFN-γ-high pre-SLE cluster is associated with higher risk of nephritis at classification, while ANA-positive cases are more likely to develop arthritis.",
      "content": "A distinct ANA-negative but IL-5/IFN-γ-high pre-SLE cluster is associated with higher risk of nephritis at classification, while ANA-positive cases are more likely to develop arthritis.\n\nSLE cluster 1 (ANA-negative, IL-5 high, IFN-γ high) showed no difference in sex, race, age, or medication history compared to ANA-positive cluster, but had higher nephritis risk (p=0.008). ANA-positive cluster had higher arthritis risk (p=0.028).",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n13"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n14",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Claim",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "Early dysregulation of Th-type cytokines IL-5 (Th2) and IL-6 (Th2/Th17) may be an essential early step in SLE pathogenesis, contributing to all stages of disease.",
      "content": "Early dysregulation of Th-type cytokines IL-5 (Th2) and IL-6 (Th2/Th17) may be an essential early step in SLE pathogenesis, contributing to all stages of disease.\n\nIL-5 and IL-6 elevated in ~20% of future SLE patients at least 6 years pre-classification and ~90% by 2 years post-classification. They were independent classifiers in most multivariate random forest models. IL-5 and IL-6 support T cell survival and antibody production, suggesting they promote autoantibody production.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n14"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n15",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Claim",
        "Claim"
      ],
      "title": "IFN-γ elevation precedes autoantibodies and drives BLyS/APRIL production, supporting a model where simultaneous dysregulation of T helper, regulatory, IFN-related, and TNF-related pathways erodes imm…",
      "content": "IFN-γ elevation precedes autoantibodies and drives BLyS/APRIL production, supporting a model where simultaneous dysregulation of T helper, regulatory, IFN-related, and TNF-related pathways erodes immune tolerance.\n\nIFN-γ elevated >3.5 years pre-classification. IFN-γ and IFN-α stimulate BLyS/APRIL production. BLyS/APRIL increase significantly within 1 year of classification. ANA positivity did not exclude IFN-γ from multivariate models, suggesting distinct roles.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n15"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n16",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Evidence",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "In pre-SLE cases >3.5 years before classification, IL-5 median normalized FI 1.38 (IQR 1.24-1.62) vs controls 0.86 (0.64-1.26), q=3.6×10⁻⁶; IL-6 1.69 (1.40-2.15) vs 1.14 (0.88-1.44), q=8.26×10⁻⁶; IFN…",
      "content": "In pre-SLE cases >3.5 years before classification, IL-5 median normalized FI 1.38 (IQR 1.24-1.62) vs controls 0.86 (0.64-1.26), q=3.6×10⁻⁶; IL-6 1.69 (1.40-2.15) vs 1.14 (0.88-1.44), q=8.26×10⁻⁶; IFN-γ 2.56 (2.20-2.98) vs 2.20 (1.74-2.75), q=0.035.\n\nFrom Table 1: median normalized fluorescence intensity values with interquartile ranges for cases (n=61) and controls (n=56) in the earliest time period, with FDR-adjusted q-values.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n16",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaut.2016.06.001"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n17",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Evidence",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Cases gained an average of 0.5 dysregulated mediators per year (p<0.001) and 0.3 autoantibody specificities per year (p<0.001), while controls gained 0.06 mediators/year and 0 autoantibody specificit…",
      "content": "Cases gained an average of 0.5 dysregulated mediators per year (p<0.001) and 0.3 autoantibody specificities per year (p<0.001), while controls gained 0.06 mediators/year and 0 autoantibody specificities/year.\n\nFrom mixed linear regression models (Table 2) and Supplemental Fig. 3/Table 2. Cases had mean 12.7 elevated mediators at classification (up from 8.8 >3.5 years prior) vs controls 6.3 (up from 5.7). Cases had mean 3.0 autoantibody specificities at classification (up from 1.0) vs controls consistently 0.1.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n17",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaut.2016.06.001"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n18",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Evidence",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "BLyS increased significantly in cases (slope 93.93 pg/ml/year) vs controls (18.38 pg/ml/year), p=0.008; APRIL increased in cases (slope 1415 pg/ml/year) vs controls (288 pg/ml/year), p=0.013, with si…",
      "content": "BLyS increased significantly in cases (slope 93.93 pg/ml/year) vs controls (18.38 pg/ml/year), p=0.008; APRIL increased in cases (slope 1415 pg/ml/year) vs controls (288 pg/ml/year), p=0.013, with significant differences only in the period <0.9 years before classification.\n\nFrom Table 2 mixed linear regression slopes and Table 1 q-values. BLyS and APRIL not different >3.5 years pre-classification (q=0.927, q=0.672) but significantly elevated 0.9 years before to 0.1 years after (q=0.003, q=0.019).",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n18",
      "sourceDocument": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaut.2016.06.001"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n19",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Evidence",
        "Evidence"
      ],
      "title": "Hazard ratios for mediator dysregulation vs autoantibody positivity: IL-5 vs anti-dsDNA 5.28 (3.56-7.82), IL-6 vs anti-dsDNA 4.22 (2.85-6.25), IFN-γ vs anti-dsDNA 3.06 (2.11-4.44), MIG vs anti-dsDNA …",
      "content": "Hazard ratios for mediator dysregulation vs autoantibody positivity: IL-5 vs anti-dsDNA 5.28 (3.56-7.82), IL-6 vs anti-dsDNA 4.22 (2.85-6.25), IFN-γ vs anti-dsDNA 3.06 (2.11-4.44), MIG vs anti-dsDNA 4.11 (2.80-6.06); all p<10⁻⁸.\n\nFrom Table 3: composite ratio of cases with elevated mediator to cases with positive autoantibody at any given time. Hazard ratio >1 indicates mediator more likely positive than autoantibody. Robust log-rank test p-values.",
      "created": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "modified": "2026-11-06T16:43:35.762Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n19",
